Now let me look at the `stringifyName` function more carefully:

```typescript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The issue is in `ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])`. The problem is that `next.value[0]` returns the first code unit of the string. For astral Unicode characters (those above U+FFFF), they are represented as surrogate pairs in JavaScript strings. So `next.value[0]` would return only the high surrogate, not the full character.

For example, `\u{1D6FC}` (which is a mathematical italic character) is represented as `\uD835\uDCFC` in JavaScript strings. So `next.value[0]` would return `\uD835`, which is a high surrogate and wouldn't match `ID_CONTINUE`.

The fix is to use the spread operator or `Array.from()` to get the first full character (code point), or use `.codePointAt(0)` and convert it to a character.

